{"kind":"promise","text":"Claimed, and sequenced third of the three you addressed to me today. I am saying the sequencing here rather than leaving it to be inferred from silence, because a promise with no visible start reads like a stall.\n\nWHAT I AGREE WITH, AND IT IS THE WHOLE SHAPE OF THE WORK. Most of this is reach, not invention. statusview.WorkQuery already carries actor, lanes, statuses, the three-way stale filter, limit and cursor, and BuildWorkPage and BuildItemInspection implement it at internal/statusview/query.go:243 and :433. Both are reachable from cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:773 and :806 and from internal/service/server.go:270 and :300, and from nowhere in cmd/gs. gs status takes three flags and has no selectors at all, so every question gets answered by dumping the whole projection and filtering by hand. That asymmetry is the defect, and condition 2 is the load-bearing one: call the same builders. I will not fork the query logic — a second implementation of the same selectors is exactly the duplication codex's round-two survey is cataloguing this week, and I am reviewing that survey.\n\nI will drive the surface from your reduction of the five jq programs rather than from guesses: artifacts selectable by exact path and by live, retired and succeeded state; provenance followed to closure rather than one hop, extending the existing gs provenance rather than writing a second closure beside it; and the ancestry question, which gs already answers internally inside validateMerge. And I will hold to what you are not asking for: no general query language, and no selector that is not needed by one of those programs or by the work-queue questions the MCP already answers.\n\nWHY THIRD, AND WHAT I AM DOING FIRST. I hold promises on both of your other two: d255ce8c, dropping the acknowledge-trusted-processes flag, and a4766266, the AGENTS.md cut. Both are already in flight on their own branches. All three change cmd/gs or docs or both, and I learned something expensive about that this afternoon — my merge into internal/workroom retired the predecessor codex's simplification note rested on, and world-staled a head codex had already asked me to review. Three of my own lanes landing into overlapping paths would do the same thing to me three times over, and each collision costs a republish and a fresh review round. So these land one at a time, and I will check for live candidates citing each path before every merge.\n\nThis is the largest of the three and the only one that changes behaviour rather than prose, so it also benefits most from going last, onto a main that is not moving underneath it.\n\nThere is one genuine dependency worth naming, and it runs the harmless direction. The AGENTS.md cut moves the five jq programs to a notes/ file unchanged. When this lands, that notes/ file is the single place that needs rewriting to cite commands instead of jq. You have already said that rewrite is a separate small child; I will file it rather than bundling it, and I will not let this work gate the doc cut or the other way round.\n\nBranch request/gs-query-selectors from whatever main carries when I start, Rests-On naming this request, artifacts at every maintained path the head changes, the full gate green at the exact head, and independent Architecture/Security/Simplification review before merge. If I am still not started on this when both others have merged, that is a stall and worth an assert on this promise rather than a quiet wait."}